{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The recorded diagnosis is refuted. The de-dup key already carries the material fingerprint.\n\nInvestigated 2026-10-27 ~21:50–22:05 PDT, before changing anything.\n\n### Three pieces of counter-evidence\n\n1. **The reproduction shows no de-dup happened.** Step 2 returned job `…dbe7ada2` and step 4 returned job `…41c67599`. A de-duplicated start *attaches to the existing job and returns its id*. Two different job ids mean the singleflight key did **not** match, so the start was not swallowed.\n2. **The key already includes the fingerprint.** `_mcp_llm._plan_review_variant` is\n   `f\"source={source or 'attested'};material={current_material_fingerprint(ticket_id)}\"`, used at both the `review_plan_start` and sync `review_plan` call sites. It was added by commit `864b6f56c91` (\"Key plan-review dedup on current plan material\", 2026-10-18, ticket `3b27-8f4b-90bd-44b2`), which is an ancestor of `origin/main`. Its own commit message describes precisely the failure this ticket reports, and it shipped nine days before this observation.\n3. **`stale-material` is not a de-dup artifact.** The only producers are `llm/plan_review/attest.py` (lines 667 and 718), inside attestation *validation*: it compares the ticket's **recorded** attestation's signed material fingerprint against the current one and returns `{\"valid\": False, \"verdict\": \"stale-material\"}`. That is why the returned `signed_at` was step 2's timestamp — it is the step-2 attestation being reported as stale, not a replayed run.\n\nSo \"the start path's de-dup key does not include the plan material fingerprint\" is false as written, and a change to that key would have fixed nothing.\n\n### What remains unexplained\n\nWhy the step-4 job produced **no findings** (`plan_reviews: 0`) and surfaced the stale-attestation verdict instead of running a fresh review, while `force=True` on the identical tree produced a real certified PASS. Two candidate mechanisms, neither yet distinguished:\n\n1. A **reuse short-circuit** fired. `llm/plan_review/__init__.py` calls `reuse.idempotent_reuse` then `reuse.verdict_reuse`, both bypassed by `force` — which matches the observed force/no-force asymmetry exactly. But on inspection neither should fire here: `idempotent_reuse` returns `None` when `claim_gate_check` is not ok (a stale attestation is not ok), and `verdict_reuse` requires the stored sidecar's `material_fingerprint` to still match. Both guards read as correct, so if one fired, the bug is in its guard, not in the key.\n2. A **precheck ahead of the LLM** returned the validation result directly. That would explain the empty findings and the replayed `signed_at` without any reuse path firing.\n\nDistinguishing these needs an instrumented run, not more reading — the two paths produce the same observable payload. I did not run one: a plan review is a billable 15–20 minute LLM operation and I could not justify spending one on a hypothesis I could not first narrow statically.\n\n### `verify_completion_start` audit (acceptance criterion 3)\n\n**It does not share the refuted plan-review defect, but its key is weaker.** Its variant at both call sites is `f\"graph={graph};source={source or 'attested'}\"` — no material fingerprint at all. The completion-verifier attestation *does* bind material (`attest.py` line 667 validates it the same way), so a completion start issued after a material edit de-duplicates onto a run that read the earlier material. That is the defect this ticket's title describes, present in `verify_completion_start` rather than in `review_plan_start`.\n\n### Recommendation\n\nRetitle and rescope around the two findings above: (a) `verify_completion_start`'s dedup variant should carry the material fingerprint, mirroring `_plan_review_variant`; (b) the plan-review symptom needs one instrumented reproduction to choose between the reuse-guard and precheck mechanisms before any code changes. Acceptance criterion 1 as written — \"does not de-dup onto a job started before that change\" — is already satisfied by `864b6f5` and should be replaced by one that names the actual observed failure: a non-forced re-review after a material edit must produce its own findings rather than reporting the superseded attestation.\n\nNo code changed for this ticket in this session."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793163629943206889,"uuid":"fe641dd1-5113-4e7b-918e-e716dc906c33"}